      "question": {
        ":question/id": "fc971d6f-1df0-5e43-b970-455ed17adce3",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x)=\\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the original quotient is defined.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}}{(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\frac{2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{x}}{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}^{2}}\\\\ &=- \\frac{2 \\left(x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}{x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
